{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Governance is a choice among ways of organizing transactions, made by comparing production costs and transaction costs.",
      "passages": ["W2042008766_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Governance is a mechanism that supplies incentives to perform when performance cannot be specified or monitored.",
      "passages": ["W2042008766_p06"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Governance varies with asset specificity and uncertainty, and bilateral governance names the dependence that arises when few parties are involved.",
      "passages": ["W2042008766_p04", "W2042008766_p05"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Governance forms are distinguished by who holds ownership and control rights over residual value.",
      "passages": ["W2042008766_p02", "W2042008766_p04"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Governance includes the joint venture as an intermediate form between contract and integration, with shared ownership and hierarchy-like administration.",
      "passages": ["W2042008766_p02"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Governance is one explanation of cooperation among several, set beside strategic positioning and organizational learning accounts rather than being the only one.",
      "passages": ["W2042008766_p07", "W2042008766_p08", "W2042008766_p01"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "extend",
    "target": "Williamson's transaction cost economics account of governance",
    "text": "The document adopts the transaction cost account of governance, adds the joint venture as an intermediate form, and places it beside strategic and learning explanations.",
    "passages": ["W2042008766_p02", "W2042008766_p03", "W2042008766_p07", "W2042008766_p08"]
  }
}

{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Governance is a selected organizational form, alongside market and hierarchy, for organizing exchange, and less efficient forms are selected out.",
      "passages": ["W2056239166_p04", "W2056239166_p01", "W2056239166_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Governance consists of adapting, coordinating and safeguarding exchanges, and forms are compared by how efficiently they perform these tasks.",
      "passages": ["W2056239166_p04", "W2056239166_p01", "W2056239166_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Network governance operates through social mechanisms, not formal contracts and authority, to coordinate and safeguard exchanges.",
      "passages": ["W2056239166_p01", "W2056239166_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Governance is explained by asset specificity, uncertainty and frequency of exchange, with behavioral uncertainty replacing opportunism as the behavioral condition.",
      "passages": ["W2056239166_p01", "W2056239166_p06", "W2056239166_p05"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Governance operates at the level of the whole network's structural embeddedness, not the dyad.",
      "passages": ["W2056239166_p02", "W2056239166_p03", "W2056239166_p04"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Governance covers a bounded, persistent set of autonomous firms organized around a goal, with membership set by relations to goal attainment, not firm attributes.",
      "passages": ["W2056239166_p08", "W2056239166_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K7",
      "text": "Network governance is distinguished from market and hierarchy as an intermediate position, and from loosely defined network accounts with vaguely specified mechanisms.",
      "passages": ["W2056239166_p02", "W2056239166_p07", "W2056239166_p01", "W2056239166_p08"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "extend",
    "target": "Williamson's transaction cost economics account of governance as comparative efficiency of exchange organization",
    "text": "The document keeps TCE's comparative-efficiency framework and adds a network form, drawing on embeddedness and task complexity while revising opportunism and the dyadic focus.",
    "passages": ["W2056239166_p02", "W2056239166_p04", "W2056239166_p05"]
  }
}

{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Legitimacy is a perception held by stakeholders of a firm's worth, meaning, predictability and trustworthiness, and it governs access to resources and exchange.",
      "passages": ["W2073260340_p02", "W2073260340_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Legitimacy is socially constructed and accumulates at both the firm level and the industry level.",
      "passages": ["W2073260340_p03", "W2073260340_p08"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Industry legitimacy serves as a possible baseline or substitute for firm legitimacy, and how the two relate is left open.",
      "passages": ["W2073260340_p08"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Legitimacy is built at the firm level through third-party media exposure (volume) and media framing (tenor), rather than through firm strategy alone.",
      "passages": ["W2073260340_p03", "W2073260340_p05"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Legitimacy is both reflected in media coverage, since some prior legitimacy makes a firm newsworthy, and added to by that coverage.",
      "passages": ["W2073260340_p01", "W2073260340_p04", "W2073260340_p05"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Lack of legitimacy is a cost to the firm, which shows up in market outcomes such as underpricing and turnover as an illegitimacy discount.",
      "passages": ["W2073260340_p06", "W2073260340_p07"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "modify",
    "target": "Prior accounts that treat media coverage as a measure of legitimacy and explain legitimacy through firm strategy or field-level processes",
    "text": "The document keeps the perception-based view of legitimacy but changes its source, casting the media as a third-party propagator that both reflects and builds legitimacy.",
    "passages": ["W2073260340_p03", "W2073260340_p04", "W2073260340_p05"]
  }
}

{"characterization":[{"id":"K1","text":"Governance is the set of private-ordering arrangements, including firms, markets and hybrid forms, that structure how contracts are carried out after a transaction is made.","passages":["W1684540946_p07","W1684540946_p08"]},{"id":"K2","text":"Governance matters most where bounded rationality, opportunism and asset specificity coincide, because planning, promise and court ordering then fail to secure contract execution.","passages":["W1684540946_p07","W1684540946_p08"]},{"id":"K3","text":"Governance structures differ in their adaptive capacities and costs, and transaction costs are economized by matching them to the differing attributes of transactions.","passages":["W1684540946_p04","W1684540946_p05"]},{"id":"K4","text":"Governance spans a continuum from discrete market exchange to centralized hierarchy, with many mixed intermediate modes between the two ends.","passages":["W1684540946_p03","W1684540946_p04","W1684540946_p08"]},{"id":"K5","text":"Governance treats the business firm as a governance structure, not merely a production function that transforms inputs into outputs.","passages":["W1684540946_p03","W1684540946_p04"]},{"id":"K6","text":"Governance in hierarchy serves efficiency purposes and not only power, which yields predictions about how work is organized.","passages":["W1684540946_p02"]},{"id":"K7","text":"Governance matters because durable firm-specific assets are widespread, which makes hit-and-run entry often infeasible.","passages":["W1684540946_p06","W1684540946_p07"]}],"stance":{"label":"unclear","target":"unclear","text":"The reader output does not show a specific account of governance that the document engages, adopts, or challenges.","passages":[]}}

