{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Governance of cooperative inter-organizational relationships is a developmental process of negotiation, commitment and execution that emerges, evolves and dissolves, not just a structure.",
      "passages": ["W1983272981_p01", "W1983272981_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Governance operates through formal structures, legal processes and informal social-psychological processes working together, such as trust, equity norms and conflict-resolution procedures.",
      "passages": ["W1983272981_p01", "W1983272981_p06"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Governance aims at efficient and equitable outcomes and at resolving internal conflict, with equity treated as equal in importance to efficiency.",
      "passages": ["W1983272981_p01", "W1983272981_p06"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Governance is needed because deals with transaction-specific investments cannot be fully specified ex ante, so formal contracts alone are insufficient.",
      "passages": ["W1983272981_p01"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Governance works through how parties negotiate, execute and modify terms, which shapes perceived equity and efficiency, motivation to continue or exit, and dispute settlement.",
      "passages": ["W1983272981_p05"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Governance relying only on formal safeguards against opportunism can backfire by eroding trust, while strong interpersonal commitment can sustain inefficient relationships.",
      "passages": ["W1983272981_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K7",
      "text": "Governance in this account applies mainly between organizations, with possible extension to units within multidivisional firms, and is distinguished from comparisons of markets, hierarchies and mixed structures.",
      "passages": ["W1983272981_p08", "W1983272981_p03", "W1983272981_p04"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "extend",
    "target": "Transaction cost and agency theories of governance, and the structural comparison literature on markets, hierarchies and mixed forms",
    "text": "The document adds social-psychological and developmental process content to transaction cost and agency accounts of governance, which it treats as incomplete rather than wrong.",
    "passages": ["W1983272981_p08", "W1983272981_p03", "W1983272981_p07"]
  }
}

{
 "characterization": [
  {
   "id": "K1",
   "text": "Governance is the formal structure of the board, captured in measurable features such as board size and the proportion of outside directors.",
   "passages": ["W2002628276_p01", "W2002628276_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K2",
   "text": "Governance is treated as a lever that affects firm financial performance, and performance is the criterion for judging whether a structure is effective.",
   "passages": ["W2002628276_p02", "W2002628276_p06"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K3",
   "text": "Governance works through monitoring and control, as independent outside directors oversee management whose interests diverge from owners'.",
   "passages": ["W2002628276_p04"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K4",
   "text": "Governance is also understood through resource dependence, in which boards supply resources, links, expertise and counsel, and this view gives different prescriptions from agency theory.",
   "passages": ["W2002628276_p03", "W2002628276_p07"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K5",
   "text": "Governance structure is prescribed and regulated by external policy bodies and advisory forums, which issue board-structure guidelines and assume that better structure improves performance.",
   "passages": ["W2002628276_p05", "W2002628276_p06"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K6",
   "text": "Governance operates at the level of the firm's board, and correlational evidence on board structure cannot show which mechanism produces its link to performance.",
   "passages": ["W2002628276_p03"]
  }
 ],
 "stance": {
  "label": "extend",
  "target": "Agency theory's board-monitoring account of governance, and its performance link, as tested in the earlier meta-analysis (Dalton et al., 1998)",
  "text": "The document adopts the structure-performance view of governance and adds a test of board independence as a moderator, while calling for agency and resource dependence views to converge.",
  "passages": ["W2002628276_p02", "W2002628276_p03", "W2002628276_p07", "W2002628276_p08"]
 }
}

{
 "characterization": [
  {
   "id": "K1",
   "text": "Legitimacy is the generalized perception or assumption that an entity's actions are desirable, proper or appropriate within a social system.",
   "passages": ["W2019422872_p03"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K2",
   "text": "Legitimacy is produced by meeting and adhering to the explicit or implicit norms, values, rules and expectations of a social system.",
   "passages": ["W2019422872_p03"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K3",
   "text": "Legitimacy is multidimensional and stakeholder-specific, including regulative legitimacy assessed by regulators and normative or public legitimacy assessed by the general public.",
   "passages": ["W2019422872_p05", "W2019422872_p06"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K4",
   "text": "Legitimacy is distinct from reputation, which is a comparison among organizations, whereas legitimacy is social acceptance from adhering to norms.",
   "passages": ["W2019422872_p01", "W2019422872_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K5",
   "text": "Legitimacy is raised by isomorphism and, at lower performance levels only, by financial performance, which gives high performers no added legitimacy.",
   "passages": ["W2019422872_p01", "W2019422872_p07", "W2019422872_p08"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K6",
   "text": "Legitimacy can be lost, questioned or challenged, and an organization judged illegitimate faces restricted market access and strategic choice from coercive or mobilizing actors.",
   "passages": ["W2019422872_p08"]
  }
 ],
 "stance": {
  "label": "extend",
  "target": "Suchman's definition of legitimacy as a generalized perception or assumption of appropriateness",
  "text": "The document adopts the perception-of-appropriateness definition and adds a distinction from reputation, with separate antecedents and stakeholder-specific dimensions.",
  "passages": ["W2019422872_p03", "W2019422872_p04", "W2019422872_p05"]
 }
}

{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Governance is the managerial choice of an organising form, market or hierarchy, for a transaction, driven by transaction attributes such as asset specificity.",
      "passages": ["W2024485756_p01"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Governance choice rests on managers' subjective perceptions of transaction costs, which is why firms with the same objective information make different make-or-buy decisions.",
      "passages": ["W2024485756_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Governance choice is conditioned by behavioural assumptions: risk preference, opportunism, bounded rationality and trust, which together shift the level of asset specificity at which firms switch forms.",
      "passages": ["W2024485756_p01", "W2024485756_p02"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Governance works through trust, which curbs opportunism, reduces behavioural uncertainty and lowers transaction costs in both markets and hierarchies.",
      "passages": ["W2024485756_p02", "W2024485756_p05", "W2024485756_p06"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Governance combines trust with control and contracts, which are interdependent and can complement or substitute for each other as the relationship develops.",
      "passages": ["W2024485756_p03", "W2024485756_p06"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Governance covers market, hierarchy and hybrid forms, and trust acts as a control mechanism across the contact, contract and execution phases of hybrid governance.",
      "passages": ["W2024485756_p01", "W2024485756_p03", "W2024485756_p06"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "extend",
    "target": "Transaction cost economics (Williamson) account of governance choice",
    "text": "The document adopts the transaction cost account of governance choice and adds trust, risk preferences and subjective managerial cost perceptions to its behavioural assumptions.",
    "passages": ["W2024485756_p01", "W2024485756_p02", "W2024485756_p07"]
  }
}

{
 "characterization": [
  {
   "id": "K1",
   "text": "Legitimacy is a generalized perception or assumption that a firm's environmental performance is desirable, proper, or appropriate.",
   "passages": ["W2056225081_p01", "W2056225081_p03"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K2",
   "text": "Legitimacy is earned when a firm's environmental performance conforms to the expectations of its stakeholders.",
   "passages": ["W2056225081_p01", "W2056225081_p03"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K3",
   "text": "Legitimacy is a judgment held by stakeholders, each applying their own norms, cognitive maps and preferences, and investors' judgments are the ones that matter for stock risk.",
   "passages": ["W2056225081_p03", "W2056225081_p02", "W2056225081_p07"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K4",
   "text": "Legitimacy is formed from available information, such as media reports and the observed actions of regulators, suppliers, customers and competitors.",
   "passages": ["W2056225081_p03", "W2056225081_p05"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K5",
   "text": "Legitimacy can be shaped by firm-issued information, such as expressions of environmental commitment and voluntary disclosure of liabilities.",
   "passages": ["W2056225081_p04", "W2056225081_p06", "W2056225081_p01", "W2056225081_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K6",
   "text": "Legitimacy works as an antecedent that lowers a firm's unsystematic stock market risk, and changes in it have an enduring effect.",
   "passages": ["W2056225081_p01", "W2056225081_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K7",
   "text": "Legitimacy is treated here only in relation to the natural environment, within an institutional theory frame.",
   "passages": ["W2056225081_p05", "W2056225081_p01", "W2056225081_p03"]
  }
 ],
 "stance": {
  "label": "extend",
  "target": "institutional theory's perceptual account of organizational legitimacy",
  "text": "The document adopts the institutional view of legitimacy as stakeholder perception and adds environmental legitimacy as a driver of stock risk, moderated by impression management.",
  "passages": ["W2056225081_p01", "W2056225081_p03", "W2056225081_p06"]
 }
}

{
 "characterization": [
  {
   "id": "K1",
   "text": "Governance is a control mechanism, or safeguard, that creates perceived fairness and supplies control and trust at minimum cost.",
   "passages": ["W2124687760_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K2",
   "text": "Governance takes varied forms of safeguard, including contracts, trust, reputation, and hostages, which are combined into a mix.",
   "passages": ["W2124687760_p03", "W2124687760_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K3",
   "text": "Governance is an up-front set-up investment that shapes ongoing transaction costs and the feasible level of specialized investment.",
   "passages": ["W2124687760_p03", "W2124687760_p07"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K4",
   "text": "Governance mediates between asset specificity and transaction costs, so costs vary with the chosen safeguards rather than with specificity alone.",
   "passages": ["W2124687760_p04", "W2124687760_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K5",
   "text": "Governance includes information sharing, the scale and scope of exchange, and repeated transacting, which reduce opportunism and contracting and bargaining costs.",
   "passages": ["W2124687760_p06", "W2124687760_p07"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K6",
   "text": "Governance affects transaction value, meaning production plus transaction costs, and so serves more than the minimization of transaction costs.",
   "passages": ["W2124687760_p07"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K7",
   "text": "Governance operates at the level of the buyer–supplier exchange relationship, and its payoff depends on specialized investments and a long-term horizon.",
   "passages": ["W2124687760_p07", "W2124687760_p01"]
  }
 ],
 "stance": {
  "label": "modify",
  "target": "Transaction cost economics (Williamson): asset specificity raises opportunism hazards and transaction costs",
  "text": "The document keeps the safeguard view of governance but argues that governance choice, not asset specificity, drives transaction costs, and that the aim is transaction value.",
  "passages": ["W2124687760_p01", "W2124687760_p04", "W2124687760_p07"]
 }
}

{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Governance in business groups is a set of internal arrangements that substitute for missing market institutions in emerging economies.",
      "passages": ["W1980934528_p01", "W1980934528_p02"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Governance works through internal labor markets and pooled recruitment that remedy failures in the managerial labor market.",
      "passages": ["W1980934528_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Governance works through umbrella brands, reputation and internal trading, backed by social and economic sanctions, to remedy failures in product and input markets.",
      "passages": ["W1980934528_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Governance includes family and social ties, which lower transaction costs whatever their origin.",
      "passages": ["W1980934528_p04"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Governance is understood by what groups do to cut transaction costs, not by formal ownership, control or decision structures.",
      "passages": ["W1980934528_p03", "W1980934528_p04"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Governance operates at the level of the business group, a confederation of legally independent firms whose members earn similar returns.",
      "passages": ["W1980934528_p01"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K7",
      "text": "Governance matters where institutions are weak, and its performance effects differ from those in advanced economies.",
      "passages": ["W1980934528_p01", "W1980934528_p02"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "contest",
    "target": "Advanced-economy view that unrelated diversification and group-like arrangements depress profit",
    "text": "The document contests the advanced-economy view by showing group affiliation raises average member profit in several emerging markets, so conclusions do not transfer across contexts.",
    "passages": ["W1980934528_p01"]
  }
}

{
 "characterization": [
  {
   "id": "K1",
   "text": "Legitimacy is an audience-conferred judgment of a category's feasibility, credibility and appropriateness, not a property that firms hold on their own.",
   "passages": ["W2091806695_p02", "W2091806695_p04"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K2",
   "text": "Legitimacy of a new market category is co-produced by entrepreneurial ventures' actions and external audiences' judgments.",
   "passages": ["W2091806695_p01", "W2091806695_p02"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K3",
   "text": "Legitimacy is built through affiliations with reputable actors, linguistic frames and collective identity claims that stabilize the category's meaning.",
   "passages": ["W2091806695_p02", "W2091806695_p06", "W2091806695_p08"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K4",
   "text": "Legitimacy is reached at a threshold of institutionalization and taken-for-grantedness, observable through broad retail availability, technological realism and rapid subscriber growth.",
   "passages": ["W2091806695_p05", "W2091806695_p06"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K5",
   "text": "Legitimacy operates mainly at the category level and is a precondition for rewarding firm-level differentiation.",
   "passages": ["W2091806695_p02", "W2091806695_p05", "W2091806695_p06", "W2091806695_p08"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K6",
   "text": "Once a category has legitimacy, audiences stop judging the category and judge firms by their alignment with the category prototype.",
   "passages": ["W2091806695_p04", "W2091806695_p05", "W2091806695_p06"]
  },
  {
   "id": "K7",
   "text": "Legitimacy is not expected to apply to fragmented or ill-defined categories, illegitimate collectives such as the Mafia, or categories under restrictions entrepreneurs cannot affect.",
   "passages": ["W2091806695_p08"]
  }
 ],
 "stance": {
  "label": "extend",
  "target": "Category-based accounts of legitimacy, in which audiences judge firms against category prototypes and identity codes",
  "text": "The document adopts the prototype-based view of audience evaluation and extends it to new categories, showing how legitimation shifts attention from the category to individual firms.",
  "passages": ["W2091806695_p04", "W2091806695_p02", "W2091806695_p06"]
 }
}

{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Governance is a control mechanism, a safeguard, whose purpose is to produce perceived fairness and trust at minimum cost.",
      "passages": ["W2124687760_p02"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Governance can rest on contracts or on self-enforcing trust, and the choice between them differs across transactors even at identical asset specificity.",
      "passages": ["W2124687760_p04", "W2124687760_p06", "W2124687760_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Governance requires up-front set-up investments that create the safeguard and in turn shape ongoing transaction costs.",
      "passages": ["W2124687760_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Governance operates through information sharing, repeated multi-component exchange, and self-enforcing safeguards, which reduce information asymmetry, opportunism, contracting costs and bargaining costs.",
      "passages": ["W2124687760_p06", "W2124687760_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Governance influences transaction value, not just transaction costs, because trustworthiness enables higher levels of performance-enhancing specialized investment.",
      "passages": ["W2124687760_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Governance is a choice shaped by institutional environment, history and preferences, not a fixed response to asset specificity.",
      "passages": ["W2124687760_p04"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K7",
      "text": "Governance is analyzed at the level of buyer-supplier exchange relationships in the auto industry, and trust-based forms pay off only where specific investments yield long-term gains.",
      "passages": ["W2124687760_p01", "W2124687760_p04", "W2124687760_p07"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "modify",
    "target": "Transaction cost economics (received TCE view of governance as safeguarding that costs more as asset specificity rises)",
    "text": "The document keeps TCE's vocabulary of safeguards and opportunism but denies that costs rise with specificity and recasts the aim as maximizing transaction value.",
    "passages": ["W2124687760_p01", "W2124687760_p02", "W2124687760_p04", "W2124687760_p07"]
  }
}

{
  "characterization": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Governance is the choice among market, contract, and hierarchy as the structure for coordinating economic activity, made to minimize transaction costs.",
      "passages": ["W2036333055_p03", "W2036333055_p04", "W2036333055_p06"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "Governance through a joint venture is hierarchical coordination in which control is shared among firms, unlike the exclusive control of a wholly owned subsidiary.",
      "passages": ["W2036333055_p06"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K3",
      "text": "Governance by equity control curbs opportunism by aligning incentives, because both sides are paid in proportion to the venture's global profits.",
      "passages": ["W2036333055_p07"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K4",
      "text": "Governance takes the form of internalization when markets fail, and scale or scope indivisibilities or management costs of acquisition make shared ownership preferable to full ownership.",
      "passages": ["W2036333055_p01", "W2036333055_p05", "W2036333055_p08"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K5",
      "text": "Governance structures are chosen by parent firms as first-best, efficiency-driven selections rather than imposed by government pressure.",
      "passages": ["W2036333055_p03"]
    },
    {
      "id": "K6",
      "text": "Governance is analyzed here at the level of the parent firms' choice of venture form, in a static framework covering domestic and international equity joint ventures.",
      "passages": ["W2036333055_p02", "W2036333055_p03"]
    }
  ],
  "stance": {
    "label": "extend",
    "target": "Williamson's transaction-cost theory of market, contract, and hierarchy governance",
    "text": "The document adopts transaction-cost theory as its starting framework and applies it to explain equity joint ventures as a shared-control form of internalization.",
